# Multi-level feedback queue

MLFQ tries to get the response time of round robin for interactive jobs and the turnaround of shortest-job-first without knowing job lengths in advance. It learns from behaviour.

## Rules (OSTEP version)

1. If A is on a higher level than B, A runs.
2. Same level: round robin.
3. A new job enters at the top level.
4. Once a job uses up its allotment at a level (no matter how many times it gave up the CPU), it moves down one level.
5. After some period S, move every job back to the top (priority boost).

Rule 4 in this form stops gaming: in the old version a job could issue an I/O just before its slice ended and stay at the top forever.

Rule 5 fixes starvation of long jobs and lets a job that became interactive climb back.

## Tuning knobs

- number of levels (we used 4 in the lab);
- quantum per level, usually longer at lower levels (10 ms, 20 ms, 40 ms, 80 ms);
- boost period S. Too long and CPU-bound jobs starve; too short and interactive jobs lose their advantage. Ousterhout called these "voo-doo constants".

## Where it shows up

Solaris TS class, the BSD scheduler and Windows NT all use variations. Linux moved to CFS instead (see cfs-notes.md).
